WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:03.510
So this is a sample of ruthenium sponge. So
the

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.419
ruthenium metal has been precipitated to form
very fine

00:00:06.419 --> 00:00:09.429
particles. So here we will just open the box

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:13.650
I am rather ashamed that I made a big mistake
about

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.099
the name of ruthenium. I thought it referred
to

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:27.400
somewhere in middle Europe perhaps in Czechoslovakia
whereas ruthenium actually comes from Ruthenia

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.939
which
is the Latin translation for the country that

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:37.040
is now called
Russia and, in fact, the element Ruthenium

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.840
was first
discovered in Russia in the city of Kazan.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.320
Why would that be a particular cause of shame
for you?

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.050
Well, because my father was born in Russia
and

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.390
therefore to get something connected to Russia
wrong is

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.730
a bit embarrassing.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.230
The ruthenium is a very, very reactive metal
especially

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.940
when it is formed in this very fine sponge
type material.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.120
Ruthenium was actually discovered relatively
early in

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:08.759
the middle of the 19th century. There is some
discussion,

00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:14.759
whether in fact the Swedish chemist Berzelius
analysed

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:17.200
something that contained ruthenium and missed
it or

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.770
not, but I am not quite sure about that. But
the reason

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.960
why I am interested in this is because I did
my

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.820
doctorate with a supervisor and if you look
at his

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:33.170
supervisor and then that Ö then go back one
supervisor

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:36.780
after another you eventually get to the Swedish
chemist

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.469
Berzelius who was my great, great, great,
great, great,

00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:43.579
great, great supervisor and you will find
that most of the

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:47.780
chemists now working in the world were related
to just a

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.560
few pioneering chemists in the 18th century.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.630
So the ruthenium is very finely divided and
if I tip it you

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.270
can see in the bottom of the vial very fine
powders but

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.619
again because of the particle size this is
really dark,

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:06.130
very black, very nice sample of ruthenium
very useful

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.369
for catalytic chemistry.

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:12.800
It is as a catalyst that ruthenium is particularly
famous.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.810
Ruthenium itself if you look at a lump of
the metal is a

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:21.810
kind of silvery colour. This is a sample of
ruthenium as a

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.250
catalyst. Now because it is an expensive metal
the

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.500
ruthenium is just distributed as a very thin
layer on

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:36.130
silica. Silica is like sand and you can see
that this finely

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:41.790
divided ruthenium looks black. They are very
finely

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:46.070
divided so it has a large surface area so
the molecules

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.420
can come in and react it. And my students
were using

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:55.030
this catalyst to react an organic compound,
that is one

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.740
with carbon and hydrogen and to get it to
react with

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:03.420
more hydrogen and so they lent me this catalyst
so I

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:10.340
could show it to you. There are other ruthenium
catalysts which dissolve in the solution that

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.620
you are
trying to get the reaction to go. And there

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.010
is a very
famous catalyst named after the American chemist

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.540
Bob
Grubbs, who won the Nobel prize about 4 years

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.810
ago,
and his catalyst does a reaction which most

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.990
people even
some chemists had not heard of called which

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:34.190
is called
metathesis in which double bonds between two

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:39.960
carbon
atoms are broken and joined together in a

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.400
different way.
And you can use this for all sorts of things

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.780
you can make
plastics out of oils; you can make new sorts

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:54.320
of wax; you
can make a really great sort of wax that has

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.090
oxygen
atoms in it; and if you use this for candles

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:04.570
then the
oxygen atoms make fragrance molecules dissolve

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:09.070
much
better than in ordinary wax. So you can have

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.730
a
Christmas candle which, when you light it,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:17.750
smells of
Christmas pudding or smells of alcohol or

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.880
vanilla or
whatever spice you want. You canít do that

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.979
with an
ordinary wax but this ruthenium catalyst opens

00:04:26.979 --> 00:04:30.590
up all
sorts of things so that is why he got the

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:31.620
Nobel Prize.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.319
He got the Nobel Prize because he made nice
smelling

00:04:36.319 --> 00:04:37.319
candles?

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:39.820
No, he got the Nobel Prize because he invented
the

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:45.770
catalyst that opened up all sorts of reactions
that

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:49.110
otherwise could not be done. Bob Grubbs compound
has

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.930
ruthenium and his is the most widely used
and it is now

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:56.870
used in a whole series of industrial processes.
This is

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:02.879
another compound of ruthenium which you can
see is a

00:05:02.879 --> 00:05:09.280
rather nice red colour and it has the rather
unromantic

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.139
name of ruthenium Bpy three times or chemists
who use

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:20.729
it call it RuBpy. And the point about this
compound is

00:05:20.729 --> 00:05:25.740
that it absorbs light well and it is very
stable, you can

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.210
shine light on it, very intense light for
a long time and

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:33.150
the compound does not decompose, it does not
change.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:37.409
But what it can do when you shine light is
that you can

00:05:37.409 --> 00:05:41.770
move electrons around inside the compound
and this

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:46.780
compound and ones like it are being used by
people to

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:51.529
try and capture the light from the sun - capture
solar

00:05:51.529 --> 00:05:52.249
energy

